Modify the Seren game in the hiyroscript/seren repository carefully and comprehensively. Do not make a superficial constants-only patch. Trace every affected gameplay path, HUD path, bot/local-player path, documentation reference, and automated check so all systems continue to agree with one another.

The goal is to rebalance the race around a new 1.00× → 3.00× speed progression, remove the old five-minute finish trigger, lengthen/rebalance boost and ultimate charging, and add skill-based ultimate-charge rewards for perfect hazard dodges and Mystery Bubble pickups.

Do not change unrelated car abilities, collision priorities, track visuals, item behavior, custom-race rules, difficulty philosophy, or finish parking behavior.

Repository architecture that matters

The current implementation is spread across several files and must be changed consistently.

js/data.js owns the shared tuning constants. It currently contains ULT_CHARGE = 75, SPEED_SECONDS = 30, MULT_STEP = 0.05, MAX_MULT = 2.00, derived MAX_TIER, RACE_MINUTES = 5, FINAL_TRACKS = 3, and FINISH_STRETCH = 900.

js/race.js owns the main race update loop. It currently computes player pace from BASE_SPEED * speedMult(), applies the existing normal boost at ×1.5, scrolls the world with d = G.speed * dt, changes speed tiers through G.speedT, starts the closing race phase after RACE_MINUTES * 60, charges the player’s ultimate, and separately applies the same boost/ultimate systems to rivals and local human racers.

js/mechanics.js contains speedMult(), ultDelta(), Mystery Bubble collection through takeBubble(), trap collision geometry, markPassed(), hazard handling, and the shared ultimate reward/penalty infrastructure.

js/hud.js currently assumes the old five-minute finish system in both raceSpan() and #raceClock. That logic must be rewritten rather than left pointing at a deleted RACE_MINUTES.

js/runtime.js currently initializes speedT with a literal 30; this must not remain stale after the new speed interval is introduced.

js/render.js scrolls visual patterns from G.scroll and has speed-streak intensity based on hard-coded speed normalization. Audit this for the new 3.00× maximum so visuals do not behave as though 2.00× were still the effective ceiling.

README.md and docs/TUNING.md explicitly describe the old 75-second ultimate, ~2.5-second boost, ~7-second refill, five-minute race structure, and 1.00×–2.00× progression. They need to match the implementation after the change.

tools/ultimate-check.mjs currently explicitly asserts ULT_CHARGE === 75 and a one-second refill of 1/75. Update it and add regression coverage for the new mechanics. Extend the existing validation/test tools rather than leaving these changes untested.

1. Change the base race speed progression to 1.00× → 3.00×

The standard road-speed progression must start at 1.00× and stop at 3.00×.

Change the tuning to:

const SPEED_SECONDS = 20;
const MULT_STEP = 0.10;
const MAX_MULT = 3.00;
const MAX_TIER = Math.round((MAX_MULT - 1) / MULT_STEP);

BASE_SPEED remains 420.

The resulting MAX_TIER should still be 20.

The progression must therefore be exactly:

0:00   1.00×
0:20   1.10×
0:40   1.20×
1:00   1.30×
...
3:20   2.00×
...
6:20   2.90×
6:40   3.00×

Every increase must be exactly +0.10×.

Never allow 3.10×, floating-point-looking HUD values such as 2.999999×, or an extra tier beyond MAX_TIER.

Keep speedMult() as the canonical source of the base race multiplier. It should continue clamping to MAX_MULT.

Do not create a second independent notion of race speed.

The base target for every racer must continue to be derived from:

BASE_SPEED * speedMult()

before personal modifiers such as ultimate, slowing, normal boost, boost cans, or shunts are applied.

Player, bots, and local human racers must all use the same progression.

2. Make world scrolling genuinely follow the new speed progression

The new multiplier must affect the actual race, not only a HUD number.

The current canonical player/world scroll path is:

target = BASE_SPEED * speedMult();
...
G.speed = ...
G.scroll += G.speed * dt;
...
const d = G.speed * dt;

Preserve that architecture.

Road markings, scenery, props, walking features, hazards, Mystery Bubbles, finish-line positioning, distance accumulation, and every other world-fixed object that currently consumes G.speed, G.scroll, or d must continue to scroll from that canonical movement.

At 3.00× base speed, the road must actually move at roughly three times the 1.00× base rate before personal modifiers.

Do not change the multiplier only visually.

Do not introduce a separate fixed scroll speed.

Audit for hidden assumptions that 2.00× is the maximum. In particular, js/render.js currently computes road-speed streak strength using hard-coded numbers:

const sr = clamp((G.speed - 330) / 620, 0, 1) + ...

Retune this normalization around BASE_SPEED and MAX_MULT, or otherwise make it suitable for the new 3.00× range so the visual acceleration still has useful progression rather than effectively saturating around the old ceiling.

Likewise, inspect any engine/audio normalization or similar hard-coded speed visualization. It is fine for effects to saturate at extreme temporary speeds from boosts/ultimates, but normal 1.00×–3.00× progression should be represented sensibly.

Keep the existing short physical speed easing unless removing it is truly necessary. The important requirement is that the canonical target and actual scrolling follow the new tier. Do not create a HUD multiplier that disagrees with the baseline race-speed system.

3. Remove the five-minute finish trigger completely

The race must no longer enter its closing phase because five minutes have elapsed.

Remove RACE_MINUTES = 5 as a finish-condition tuning value and remove all gameplay/HUD dependencies on it.

G.raceT may remain. It is useful as elapsed-race time and is used elsewhere for animations/projections. Do not delete raceT just because the five-minute rule disappears.

The old logic in js/race.js currently does approximately this:

if (G.tracksLeft < 0 && G.raceT >= RACE_MINUTES * 60)
  G.tracksLeft = FINAL_TRACKS;

That condition must disappear.

Do not replace five minutes with another arbitrary elapsed-time threshold.

4. Start the final-track/finish-line sequence only after 3.00× is reached

For race modes that run to a flag (bots and local through the existing toFlag() behavior), the closing sequence must become eligible only once the normal speed progression has reached 3.00×.

The correct lifecycle is:

Race starts
→ speed progresses from 1.00× to 3.00×
→ exactly when MAX_TIER / 3.00× is reached, arm the existing FINAL_TRACKS phase
→ existing track transitions continue
→ after the existing final-track countdown reaches zero, plant the finish line
→ FINISH_STRETCH remains 900 metres
→ racers finish and park exactly as they do now

Use the existing G.tracksLeft, FINAL_TRACKS, switchTrack(), G.finishAt, FINISH_STRETCH, checkFinish(), and parking system instead of building a second finish mechanism.

A good authoritative place for the new trigger is immediately after the speed-tier update in the running-race clock block:

if (
  toFlag() &&
  G.finished === null &&
  G.tracksLeft < 0 &&
  G.tier >= MAX_TIER
) {
  G.tracksLeft = FINAL_TRACKS;
}

The exact structure can vary, but it must be a one-time state transition associated with reaching MAX_TIER.

Do not repeatedly reset G.tracksLeft to FINAL_TRACKS every frame.

Do not plant the finish line immediately at 3.00×. Reaching 3.00× starts the existing final-track system. The finish line must still come through that system and the existing FINISH_STRETCH.

Keep FINAL_TRACKS = 3 and FINISH_STRETCH = 900 unless another existing invariant requires a structural rewrite.

Endless mode must remain endless. Reaching 3.00× in Endless must never create tracksLeft, finishAt, or a finish line.

5. Rewrite the HUD race projection for the new finish rule

js/hud.js::raceSpan() currently projects the end of the race using the time remaining until the five-minute mark. That becomes invalid.

Rewrite the projection around time remaining until MAX_TIER.

Before 3.00×, determine the remaining speed-progression time from G.tier and G.speedT.

For example, conceptually:

const tiersRemaining = MAX_TIER - G.tier;
const secondsToMax =
  tiersRemaining <= 0
    ? 0
    : G.speedT + Math.max(0, tiersRemaining - 1) * SPEED_SECONDS;

Then account for the track timer as it will stand when the 3.00× threshold is reached and project the existing closing tracks plus FINISH_STRETCH.

The old raceSpan() already contains logic that advances G.trackT conceptually to a future race milestone. Reuse that idea, replacing the old five-minute milestone with the new 3.00× milestone.

Once G.tracksLeft >= 0, continue using the actual final-track state.

Once G.finishAt exists, use the real finish distance exactly as today.

Endless must still return no fixed finish span.

6. Replace the obsolete five-minute HUD clock with a speed readout

The current #raceClock displays the five-minute countdown before the final phase. That countdown must disappear.

Before the final-track sequence begins, use that HUD space to display the current base race multiplier to two decimals:

1.00×
1.10×
1.20×
...
3.00×

It must come from the same canonical multiplier as gameplay, preferably speedMult(), not from a duplicated formula.

When the closing-track phase begins, preserve the useful final-race information, but keep the speed state understandable. A compact format such as these is appropriate:

3.00× · T-3
3.00× · T-2
3.00× · T-1
3.00× · 842m

The exact separator can match Seren’s existing HUD style, but do not display the removed five-minute countdown anywhere.

Avoid adding a large new HUD component if the existing #raceClock can cleanly become the speed/final-state readout.

7. Increase shared ultimate charge time from 75 seconds to 85 seconds

Change:

const ULT_CHARGE = 75;

to:

const ULT_CHARGE = 85;

This must remain the one shared charging clock for every racer.

The player and every rival/local human already charge using:

dt / ULT_CHARGE

Preserve that shared architecture.

One second of normal charge should therefore add exactly approximately:

1 / 85 = 0.0117647...

to the normalized 0–1 ultimate meter.

A completely empty ultimate meter takes 85 seconds of eligible charging to reach 100%.

Do not change:

ULT_TIME = 15
ULT_SPEED = 2.0

The active ultimate should still last 15 seconds and still provide its existing 2× personal pace multiplier.

Update stale comments saying “seventy-five seconds.”

Update the hard-coded tests in tools/ultimate-check.mjs from 75 and 1/75 to 85 and 1/85.

8. Add +10 percentage points of ultimate charge for a true perfect hazard dodge

Add a skill reward for a genuine last-second hazard dodge.

A successful perfect dodge grants:

+0.10

to the racer’s normalized ultimate meter, i.e. +10 percentage points.

Introduce a named tuning constant rather than burying the reward inline, for example:

const ULT_ON_PERFECT_DODGE = 0.10;

The timing window should be intentionally very small. Use approximately:

const PERFECT_DODGE_WINDOW = 0.12;

meaning roughly 120 milliseconds of genuine imminent danger.

The important design requirement is that this must not become a generic “passed near a hazard” bonus.

A racer should qualify only when the hazard was on a legitimate collision course within that tiny imminent-impact window and the racer then avoids the impact through last-second lateral movement.

Use Seren’s real collision geometry. Do not implement this with lane numbers alone.

The game already has useful primitives such as carHit(), nearestOnCar(), puddleHits(), sweptY(), the trap radius values, and the per-racer hazard bit system used by o.hit, o.nm, and markPassed().

Build the perfect-dodge system around those existing primitives.

A robust design is to maintain small per-hazard bitmasks such as a “perfect-dodge threat” mask and “perfect-dodge already rewarded” mask. Player bit 1 and the existing rival bits (2 << index) can be reused so one shared hazard can independently track all racers without creating a parallel player-only implementation.

Only arm the threat bit if the racer’s pre-avoidance trajectory/hull would genuinely collide with the hazard within PERFECT_DODGE_WINDOW.

Then, when that hazard safely passes that racer or resolves without hitting them, grant the reward only if that threat bit was armed and the racer actually escaped the threatened collision.

Use swept/predicted geometry so a 120 ms window does not become frame-rate dependent. The main frame loop caps dt, but the mechanic should still work at 30, 60, 120+ FPS and under occasional long frames.

For road hazards such as puddles and tumbleweeds, use their actual shape/radius and relative movement.

For meteors, treat the imminent rock/impact/blast timing consistently with the existing rockLead(), rockAlt(), falling timer, and detonation geometry. A racer that genuinely exits the danger footprint during the tiny final window may earn the reward; simply being nowhere near the meteor must not.

A normal early lane change must not qualify.

A wide miss must not qualify.

Driving beside a hazard without making a last-second avoidance must not qualify.

Getting hit must not qualify.

The same hazard must never award the same racer twice.

Do not award perfect-dodge charge to a racer that is wrecked, finished, invulnerable, outside the shared road in Rhosyn’s Aero-Glow, or airborne as Saffron where normal road-hazard interaction is intentionally absent.

The mechanic should work fairly for the player, local human racers, and bots. Difficulty may affect whether a bot is capable of making such a maneuver through its normal driving intelligence, but difficulty must not multiply the reward.

Grant the reward through the existing:

ultDelta(who, ULT_ON_PERFECT_DODGE);

infrastructure.

That is important because ultDelta() already preserves Seren’s invariant that charge rewards/penalties do not extend or shorten an ultimate that is currently active.

Do not add +10% directly to G.ult or R.ult in a way that bypasses that invariant.

9. Mystery Bubble collection must grant +5 percentage points of ultimate charge

Every successfully collected Mystery Bubble must now grant the collecting racer:

+0.05

ultimate charge, i.e. +5 percentage points.

Add a named constant such as:

const ULT_ON_BUBBLE = 0.05;

and apply it through:

ultDelta(who, ULT_ON_BUBBLE);

The important repository-specific detail is that Mystery Bubble item rewards are currently intentionally disabled:

const MYSTERY_ITEMS_ENABLED = false;

Do not turn this back on.

The new +5 ultimate-charge reward must work independently of the disabled item-reward gate.

takeBubble() currently returns immediately when MYSTERY_ITEMS_ENABLED is false. Therefore apply the ultimate-charge reward before that early return.

Conceptually:

function takeBubble(who, bx, by) {
